\section{Choose a Logic and a Program Fragment}

Robert S. Boyer and J Strother Moore's \textit{Proving Theorems
About LISP Functions} uses a first-order, side-effect-free
fragment. A formula can be written as a Lisp expression with
symbols standing for universally quantified inputs. Evaluation
unfolds a recursive definition when its argument's shape is
known; when it cannot do so, the blocked selector can suggest
the structure on which to induct. A fixed test input would
always have a known shape and would never leave this sort of
proof obligation. The method assumes that the functions under
consideration terminate; that assumption is not a termination
proof for arbitrary Lisp definitions.

Before using such an equation as a rewrite, specify the
observations it protects. Two programs producing structurally
equal lists need not return the same cell to a caller testing
identity. They may also share different suffixes with an input
that later mutates. A proof for finite pure lists does not
establish the same theorem for cyclic cons graphs, effects,
or errors on inputs outside the intended domain.

\section{Worked Proof: Reversal With an Accumulator}

Write \(c(h,t)\) for a cons, \(nil\) for the empty list, and
\(u\mathbin{+\!\!+}v\) for append. Let \(R\) denote reversal
by appending the head after reversing the tail. Let \(F\)
move each head from its first argument to its accumulator:
\[
\begin{aligned}
  R(nil)&=nil, &
  R(c(h,t))&=R(t)\mathbin{+\!\!+}[h],\\
  F(nil,a)&=a, &
  F(c(h,t),a)&=F(t,c(h,a)).
\end{aligned}
\]
The desired equation \(F(x,nil)=R(x)\) gives an induction
hypothesis too weak for the recursive call, whose accumulator
is nonempty. Prove instead \(F(x,a)=R(x)\mathbin{+\!\!+}a\)
for every finite proper list \(a\). The empty case follows
from the definition of append. In the cons case, instantiate
the induction hypothesis on \(t\) with accumulator \(c(h,a)\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
 F(c(h,t),a)
   &= F(t,c(h,a))\\
   &= R(t)\mathbin{+\!\!+}c(h,a)\\
   &= (R(t)\mathbin{+\!\!+}[h])\mathbin{+\!\!+}a\\
   &= R(c(h,t))\mathbin{+\!\!+}a .
\end{aligned}
\]
The third line uses append associativity and the definition
of singleton append. Associativity itself follows by
induction on append's first argument. The first argument to
\(F\) decreases at every step, so the proof applies to a
terminating computation. For example,
\(F([p,q],[r])=[q,p,r]\), not merely \([q,p]\).
Phillip D. Summers's \textit{A Methodology for LISP Program
Construction from Examples} describes deriving recurrences
from regularities between examples and generalizing by adding
a parameter. The recurrence suggested by a finite set of
examples still needs an argument like the one above.

Robin Jones, Clive Maynard, and Ian Stewart present proof as a
practical concern in \textit{The Art of Lisp Programming}. For
this example the equation is useful when changing the algorithm:
on a list of length \(n\), reversal by append copies spines of
lengths from \(0\) to \(n-1\), while \(F\) allocates one result
cell per element. The proof establishes the same sequence of
elements, not the same allocation trace. Nor does tail position
promise constant stack space in all Common Lisp implementations.
Both costs need a separate argument under the chosen implementation
model; actual runtime measurements are a further step.

\section{Prove the Implementation Against the Semantics}

Malcolm C. Newey's \textit{Formal Semantics of Lisp with
Applications to Program Correctness} uses LCF in work on
Lisp evaluation and develops theories for a Lisp subset and
a PDP-10 Lisp assembly language. A compiler correctness
theorem relates two semantics; it is not simply another
source-level list equation. Joshua Guttman, Vipin Swarup,
and John Ramsdell's \textit{VLISP: A Verified Implementation
of Scheme} connects Scheme's denotational semantics with
bytecode execution through a series of refinements, including
the implementation of garbage collection.

W. Richard Stark's \textit{LISP, Lore, and Logic} treats equations
over a Lisp fragment as reductions. Sound reductions can prove
equivalence without providing a procedure that decides every
equivalence question. Gregory Chaitin's \textit{Elegant Lisp
Programs} makes the gap especially concrete. A single shorter
program returning the same output refutes minimality, but a
proof of minimality must rule out \emph{all} shorter programs,
including ones that may fail to halt. In his deliberately chosen
Lisp model this yields an incompleteness result, not a practical
method for finding the shortest equivalent program.

Magnus O. Myreen and Michael J. C. Gordon's verified Lisp
implementations prove parsing, evaluation and printing for
a core of Lisp 1.5 down to ARM, x86 and PowerPC machine code
in HOL4, building on verified allocation and collection.
One useful intermediate assertion is that changing heap
addresses preserves the abstract objects denoted by live
values, the invariant discussed in Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-157}.
It would be unsound for a compiler proof to use that assertion
if any reachable pointer could remain stale after collection.
Chapter~29 of \textit{The Modern Compiler Dossier} proposes
making comparable assumptions explicit as checkable rewrite
contracts rather than implicit obligations of every pass.
