\section{Checking a Stack-Code Certificate With Symbolic Entries}

A small translation validator makes the distinction between
source and machine semantics concrete. Use source expressions
with exact integer constants, variables, and subtraction; the
straight-line stack machine has load, push, and subtract.
Each load reads an immutable environment, and subtraction
uses unbounded mathematical integers. The validator's inputs
are a source tree and candidate bytecode, and its result is
acceptance or rejection based on symbolic interpretation.
This is an independent exercise inspired by refinement-based
verification, not a replica of VLISP's proof machinery. It
checks operand order, underflow, and final stack size without
testing any particular integer environment.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input source tree $e$, candidate instruction list $C$
  \Output accepted structural certificate or rejection
  \State $S\gets$ empty symbolic stack
  \ForAll{instruction $i$ in $C$}
    \If{$i$ is load or push with a valid operand}
      \State push its variable or literal tree onto $S$
    \ElsIf{$i$ is subtract and $|S|\ge2$}
      \State $r\gets\Call{Pop}{S}$ \Comment{Right operand first}
      \State $l\gets\Call{Pop}{S}$
      \State push $\mathsf{Sub}(l,r)$ onto $S$
    \Else
      \State \Return rejection \Comment{Cannot certify this step}
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $S=[e]$
\end{sudocode}

Take the source $(x-2)-y$. Candidate code loads $x$, pushes $2$,
subtracts, loads $y$, then subtracts again. The symbolic stack
starts with $[x,2]$ just before the first subtraction, becomes
$[\mathsf{Sub}(x,2)]$, and ends with exactly
$[\mathsf{Sub}(\mathsf{Sub}(x,2),y)]$. The validator accepts.
For $x=9$ and $y=3$, both evaluations yield $4$. A candidate
loading $y$ first, then evaluating $x-2$ and subtracting,
leaves $\mathsf{Sub}(y,\mathsf{Sub}(x,2))$ instead; it is
rejected and the same environment yields $-4$.

\Cref{fig:visual-159} compares the two candidates at the last
instruction. Both pass a stack-height check, but only one passes
the symbolic check.

